/-
  RelSem.CorpusBFiles — V0 (2026-08-27): THE BATCH-B CORPUS PROGRAM
  FAMILIES (the FROZEN target corpus's memory-input rows — P04/P05/
  P06/P14/P15; record docs/2026-08-27_v0-statements-and-ban.md).

  THE FAMILY MECHANISM (mkHarness lineage, arc-15 template, at the
  Core-AST level): each program's FIXED CODE (designated function +
  `main`) is emitted verbatim from the pinned oracle dumps
  (RelSem/CorpusBCore.lean, drift-gated); the SPLICED globals
  (`choices[]`/`expected*`/the arena) are built PARAMETRICALLY here —
  `pXXFileOf m` is the Core file of the harness instance at model
  `m`, and the statements quantify over the family (the corpus doc's
  `∀ m, wf m → exec(mkHarness(encode m, encode(modelFn m))) ⇓
  Specified 0` shape, at the file level — the speclab
  `divmodI8FileOf` pattern generalized to array splices).

  DELIBERATE, DOCUMENTED SIMPLIFICATION (validated behaviorally at
  the sample points, the speclab precedent): the oracle's elaborated
  global initializers carry per-element evaluation ceremony (unseq
  binders, conv_loaded_int wrappers, the unary-minus
  catch_exceptional_condition_sub pattern for negative literals);
  the builders splice the ALREADY-EVALUATED loaded values
  (`Specified(Array(Specified v, …))`) into the same
  create/store(_lock)/pure glob spine — semantically equal for
  in-range values (which every statement's wf demands), and the
  store path re-checks type compatibility at runtime (loud on any
  mismatch). The corpus sample instances execute to their oracle
  verdicts through these terms (EmitLeanCoreTest corpusBPoints).

  House rules: no sorry, no axioms. Under the in-build audit.
-/

import Core_run_aux
import CerbFS
